 "drivingQuestion": "Why do the Rabbis return a gift in the Jubilee?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "bekhorot.52b.7",
   "daf": "52b",
   "sentenceId": 6,
   "he": "וַחֲכָמִים אוֹמְרִים: מַתָּנָה כְּמֶכֶר. מַאי טַעְמַיְיהוּ דְּרַבָּנַן? ״תָּשׁוּבוּ״ — לְרַבּוֹת אֶת הַמַּתָּנָה.",
   "en": "The mishna further teaches: «And the Rabbis say:» The halakhic status of «a gift» is «like» that of «a sale,» and must be returned. The Gemara asks: «What is the reason of the Rabbis?» The Gemara answers that the term «“you shall return”» in the verse: “In the Jubilee Year you shall return every man to his possession” (Leviticus 25:13), is superfluous, as it is already stated: “It shall be a Jubilee for you, and you shall return every man to his possession” (Leviticus 25:10). Therefore, this term serves «to include a gift,» which is also returned in the Jubilee Year."
  }
 ],
